re- and post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8ec1f0b277e74c0f\",\"cmd-bf27240e208f40ce\",\"cmd-381cee6d549f4811\",\"cmd-bec28fa883db44a8\",\"cmd-dc1a4c1a9ce64146\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 forwards all other queries upstream\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"google.com, example.com and other external names resolve correctly through 10.100.187.10 via the configured 1.1.1.1/9.9.9.9 name-servers, verified both before and after node1's restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bf27240e208f40ce\",\"cmd-bec28fa883db44a8\",\"cmd-dc1a4c1a9ce64146\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 configured to resolve through node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"resolv.conf on node2/node3 shows nameserver 10.100.187.10 (set via NetworkManager/nmcli), and getent hosts confirms both inventory.internal and external names resolve correctly through node1, before and after their restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-db645e26f3534582\",\"cmd-a99fb5cc669340b5\",\"cmd-e9581eb568ec446c\",\"cmd-10715eec32c543e7\",\"cmd-a0763171d08347e3\",\"cmd-d7b5a07de5884dab\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Implementation done via Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible playbook (dns.yml) run from the node4 control node configures node1's VyOS DNS forwarding (via become+shell to work around VyOS's non-interactive configure-session quirks) and node2/node3's DNS via community.general.nmcli; the final run is clean, idempotent, and re-verified after a control-node restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-83aba4900eb04dd9\",\"cmd-fb774e3e7bf542ae\",\"cmd-6ca6695ec25b4fcf\",\"cmd-9975b04810f74a93\",\"cmd-0a36f67949a44d26\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"VyOS DNS configuration persists across restart (save/config.boot)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"config.boot on node1 contains the full `service dns forwarding` block including the authoritative-domain and name-server entries, and the configuration and DNS service were confirmed intact and functioning after node1's reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-11fe538a94e44b9b\",\"cmd-34cc8353ae074bd5\",\"cmd-dc1a4c1a9ce64146\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.6,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map cleanly to prerequisites (SSH key setup), the manual VyOS DNS probing needed to discover correct syntax, or the final Ansible-applied configuration. The manual test config on node1 (cmd-6934b250ea89449e, cmd-bb619907d0d145da, cmd-70d4565cecff4a89, cmd-8ec1f0b277e74c0f) was fully reverted (cmd-1fc29940e8fd4229) before the Ansible-managed config was applied, which is acceptable troubleshooting. However, node1's /root/.ssh/authorized_keys was populated twice (cmd-10fead80dfd14a4d, then again at cmd-f5f80ef25dcc400c) based on a mistaken assumption that root SSH would work; the first copy was removed (cmd-6cc8cf67948f47a9) but the second was left in place with no evidence of cleanup — low risk since PermitRootLogin is disabled on node1, making the key inert, but it is unreverted residue with no functional purpose in the final solution. More significantly, the after-executor lifecycle snapshot shows NetworkManager-wait-online.service moved to a failed state on both node2 and node3 after their restarts, which was not present in the before-prepare baseline; this is plausibly a side effect of the nmcli dns4_ignore_auto configuration and was not diagnosed or remediated by the executor.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-10fead80dfd14a4d\",\"cmd-f5f80ef25dcc400c\",\"cmd-6cc8cf67948f47a9\",\"cmd-1fc29940e8fd4229\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The material outcomes of the task were fully achieved and directly verified: node1 (VyOS) authoritatively answers inventory.internal with node2's address and forwards all other queries upstream, node2 and node3 are configured (via Ansible/nmcli) to resolve through node1, the implementation is delivered as an idempotent Ansible playbook, and the VyOS configuration persists in config.boot. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all four nodes with clear pre-restart baselines, changed boot identities, and confirmed post-restart recovery, including DNS functionality and playbook idempotency/control-plane connectivity after node4's own reboot. The main blemish is a failed NetworkManager-wait-online.service on node2 and node3 that appeared only after the restart (absent in the baseline), likely linked to the DNS/nmcli configuration change, plus a small unreverted, low-risk leftover SSH key file on node1 that has no practical effect since root password/pubkey login is disabled there.\",\"limitations\":[\"NetworkManager-wait-online.service failing on node2 and node3 post-restart is visible only in the fixed lifecycle snapshots, not diagnosed or explained in the executor's own command trace, so its root cause (likely the ignore_auto_dns4 nmcli setting) is inferred rather than directly confirmed.\",\"It is not confirmed from the trace whether the second copy of /root/.ssh/authorized_keys on node1 (added at cmd-f5f80ef25dcc400c) was ever cleaned up, though it appears functionally inert given PermitRootLogin is disabled.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 (VyOS) is authoritative for inventory.internal, answering with node2's address","status":"satisfied","summary":"dig against 10.100.187.10 for inventory.internal consistently returns 10.100.187.11 (node2), configured via `service dns forwarding authoritative-domain inventory.internal records a @ address 10.100.187.11` and confirmed both pre- and post-restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8ec1f0b277e74c0f","cmd-bf27240e208f40ce","cmd-381cee6d549f4811","cmd-bec28fa883db44a8","cmd-dc1a4c1a9ce64146"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node1 forwards all other queries upstream","status":"satisfied","